8.text:
The flag of Tonga consists of a red field containing a white canton (upper hoist-side rectangle) that is itself charged with a red Greek cross. Consequently, after rasterization to an \(m \times n\) pixel grid, every pixel is colored either red or white; the associated matrix \(M\) therefore takes only two distinct scalar values \(a\) (red) and \(b\) (white).

All rows of \(M\) belong to one of at most four combinatorial families, determined by the vertical position relative to the canton and the cross:

- rows lying entirely outside the canton are constant-\(a\) vectors;
- rows intersecting the canton but missing the cross consist of a contiguous block of \(b\)'s followed by a contiguous block of \(a\)'s;
- rows intersecting the vertical arm of the cross consist of two blocks of \(b\)'s separated by a block of \(a\)'s, followed by a trailing block of \(a\)'s;
- rows intersecting the horizontal arm of the cross consist of a single contiguous block of \(a\)'s (the arm spans essentially the whole canton width) followed by a trailing block of \(a\)'s.

Hence the row space of \(M\) is spanned by at most four distinct vectors. For generic choices \(a \neq b\) (e.g., \(a=1\), \(b=0\)) these four vectors are linearly independent, so the bound is attained and \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=4\).

No larger rank is possible, because any finer pixelation merely replicates one of the four row patterns already enumerated.

