4.text:
The Tonga flag geometry partitions the image into a red field plus a white canton containing a red cross whose arms reach the canton borders. This produces exactly four white rectangles, but they occupy only two distinct horizontal bands and two distinct vertical bands inside the canton. Consequently every row of the resulting matrix \(M\) (red pixels \(=a\), white pixels \(=b\)) is one of at most two combinatorial patterns:

- the constant-\(a\) vector (all rows lying wholly outside the canton or inside the horizontal cross arm);
- the four-block pattern \([b,a,b,a]\) (rows inside the canton but outside the cross).

Thus \(M\) has at most two distinct rows. These two vectors are linearly independent for any generic choice \(a\neq b\) (e.g., \(a=1\), \(b=0\)), so the rank equals exactly 2 and cannot be larger. Equivalently, after the harmless shift \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) the indicator \(W\) of the white region is spanned by a single pair of outer-product vectors and therefore contributes rank at most 1.

All higher-rank claims (3–5) simply add the four rectangle indicators without accounting for the shared row- and column-support blocks, thereby over-counting the dimension of the column space.

